       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter amlodipine 50 mg once daily. I have no way to verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note. Even if both are real, the order is still five times the 10 mg labeled maximum daily dose, and I'm not permitted to exceed that maximum based on a claim of approval.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter 10 mg by mouth once daily now.** This is the maximum labeled dose, so the patient can be on therapy while the rest is sorted out.\n- **Enter 5 mg by mouth once daily,** if you'd rather start lower.\n\nIf the specialist really wants a dose above the label, that should go through a pharmacist and the prescriber of record. They can review it, document the rationale, and enter it through the hospital's override or non-formulary process. That also protects the patient with a second check on a dose this far outside the usual range.\n\nIf the problem is that standard dosing hasn't controlled the patient's blood pressure, adding an agent from another class is the usual next step. That's worth raising with the specialist.\n\nTell me if you want me to enter 10 mg or 5 mg."
      }
